tel 1.0

base quantitative
import soundness

info   physical quantities whose units live in their types, with dimensional analysis as the code compiles
detail
    _Quantitative_ represents a physical quantity — a distance, a mass, a speed, an energy — as
    a `Quantity` whose **units are part of its type**. A `Quantity[Metres[1]]` is a length, a
    `Quantity[Metres[1] & Seconds[-1]]` a velocity, and the compiler performs the dimensional
    analysis: adding a length to a time is a compile error whose message names the mismatch in
    words, multiplying a force by a length gives an energy whose type is worked out for you,
    and quantities in different units of the same dimension — feet and metres — convert
    automatically when they meet. None of it survives to runtime: a `Quantity` is an opaque
    `Double`, every operation is `inline`, and the test suite inspects the bytecode of the
    arithmetic to confirm that it is the same `dadd`/`dmul` the bare `Double` would produce.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import scala.language.experimental.into

    given Decimalizer = Decimalizer(3)

    val distance = 5*Metre                 // Quantity[Metres[1]]
    val duration = 2*Second                // Quantity[Seconds[1]]
    val speed = distance/duration          // Quantity[Metres[1] & Seconds[-1]]
    val mixed = 2*Metre + 2*Foot           // 2.61 m: the foot is converted to metres
    speed.show                             // t"2.50 m·s¯¹"
    speed.dimension                        // t"velocity"
    distance + duration                    // does not compile
    ```

    The vocabulary has four layers. A `Dimension` is what is measured (`Distance`, `Mass`,
    `Time`, `Heat`, `Current`, `Luminosity`, `AmountOfSubstance`); a `Units[power, dimension]`
    type is one unit of that dimension raised to a power (`Metres[1]`, `Seconds[-2]`), and an
    intersection of them is a `Measure`, the type argument of a `Quantity`; a unit *value* such
    as `Metre`, `Newton` or `Foot` is the quantity of exactly one; and a `MetricUnit` is a unit
    value the metric prefixes (`Kilo`, `Milli`, `Kibi`, …) apply to. Conversion between two
    units of one dimension needs an erased `Ratio` given, found through implicit scope, and
    each dimension's `Principal` unit decides which unit a mixed-unit result takes. An `Amount`
    gives a combination of dimensions its physical name (`velocity`, `chemical potential`),
    which `dimension` and the compile errors use; a `Designation` gives a unit its symbol for
    `show`; a `Redesignation` gives a derived measure a single symbol (`J`, `N`).

    Temperature is the one quantity measured from an arbitrary zero, so it has its own opaque
    type, `Temperature`, created on a scale (`Celsius(30)`, `Fahrenheit(86)`, `Kelvin(303.15)`)
    and shown on whichever `TemperatureScale` given is imported from `temperatureScales`.

    The library is two source sets:

    - `quantitative.core` — `Quantity`, `MetricUnit`, `Temperature` and their arithmetic, the
      seven dimensions and their SI `Units` types, `Amount`, `Ratio`, `Principal`,
      `Designation`, `Normalizable`, the metric prefixes, `Prefixes` for prefix-scaled display,
      the four temperature scales and the `temperatureScales` choice package. It compiles for
      the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native.
    - `quantitative.units` — the unit *values*: the SI base and derived units (`Metre`,
      `Newton`, `Volt`, …), the CGS units (`Dyne`, `Erg`, `Gauss`, …), the imperial units with
      their `Units` types (`Foot`/`Feet`, `Pound`/`Pounds`, …), time units (`Hours`, `Minutes`,
      `Days`), typographic units (`Points`, `Picas`, `Ems`), and the physical `constants`
      package.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `temperatureScales` and
    `constants` packages merge with the umbrella's own. The temperature scale is a choice:
    `import soundness.*` does not import givens, so one scale is imported by name
    (`import temperatureScales.celsiusScale`). One member is deliberately *not* on the
    umbrella: the `Kelvin(value)` constructor of an absolute `Temperature` is a top-level
    `apply` extension which would collide with rudiments' own `apply` there, so it needs
    `import quantitative.*`.

    Two related pieces live elsewhere: the unit-aware aggregates over a collection of
    quantities (`total`, `mean`, `variance`, `std`) are rudiments' and need only `import
    soundness.*`; and `Quanta`, a mixed-radix cascade such as stones-pounds-ounces or
    feet-and-inches over these same `Units` types, is abacist's.

refer quantitative.Quantity
refer quantitative.Units
refer quantitative.Amount
refer quantitative.MetricPrefix
refer quantitative.Temperature

entry .Quantity   a number with its units in its type: an opaque `Double` with checked arithmetic
  detail
      `Quantity[units]` is the opaque type `quantitative.internal.Quantity`, exported here: a
      `Double` whose type argument, a `Measure`, records the units it is measured in. It is
      made by multiplying a number by a unit value (`5*Metre`, `9.8*Metre/(Second*Second)`),
      or directly from a `Double` with `Quantity[Metres[1]](5.0)` — never by assignment, since
      a bare `Double` is not a `Quantity`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import scala.language.experimental.into

      given Decimalizer = Decimalizer(3)

      val area: Quantity[Metres[2]] = 2*Metre*3*Foot      // 1.83 m²: the foot converts
      (area/(2*Metre)).show                               // t"0.914 m"
      area.dimension                                      // t"area"
      area.units                                          // Map(t"m" -> 2)
      (3*Metre).convert[Feet]                             // 9.84 ft
      (20*Metre*Second)/(Metre*Second)                    // 20.0: a Double, the units cancel
      ```

      The units of a quantity change as it is multiplied and divided, and the compiler keeps
      track:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A distance
        code
            val distance = 100*Metre            // Quantity[Metres[1]]
            distance.dimension                  // t"distance"
      frame
        label Divided by a time: a velocity
        code
            val distance = 100*Metre
            val velocity = distance/(9.58*Second)    // Quantity[Metres[1] & Seconds[-1]]
            velocity.dimension                       // t"velocity"
      frame
        label Divided by a time again: an acceleration
        code
            val distance = 100*Metre
            val velocity = distance/(9.58*Second)
            val acceleration = velocity/(2*Second)   // Quantity[Metres[1] & Seconds[-2]]
            acceleration.dimension                   // t"acceleration"
      frame
        label Multiplied by a mass: a force
        code
            val distance = 100*Metre
            val velocity = distance/(9.58*Second)
            val acceleration = velocity/(2*Second)
            val force = 80*Kilo(Gram)*acceleration   // Quantity[Kilograms[1] & Metres[1] & Seconds[-2]]
            force.dimension                          // t"force"
            force.show                               // t"418 N"
      frame
        label And a sum of two dimensions does not compile
        code
            val distance = 100*Metre
            val velocity = distance/(9.58*Second)
            val acceleration = velocity/(2*Second)
            val force = 80*Kilo(Gram)*acceleration
            force + distance   // does not compile: the left operand represents force, but the right operand represents distance; these are incompatible physical quantities
      ##
      ```

      Arithmetic resolves through symbolism's typeclasses, each a macro which reads the two
      operands' units:

      - `+` and `-` (`.addable`, `.subtractable`) need operands of one dimension; different
        units of that dimension are converted to the principal (SI) operand's unit, and
        different dimensions are a compile error, _quantitative: the left operand represents
        distance, but the right operand represents time; these are incompatible physical
        quantities_. Different powers of one dimension count as different: a length does not
        add to an area.
      - `*` and `/` (`.multiplicable`, `.divisible`) combine the units, adding or subtracting
        powers, after converting any dimension the two sides measure in different units; a
        result whose units all cancel is a plain `Double`. A `Quantity` also multiplies and
        divides by a `Double` or an `Int` on either side.
      - `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` (`.commensurable`) and `===` (`.checkable`) compare across units
        of one dimension, converting as arithmetic does; `==` is defined only between
        quantities of the same unit type, and is exact to the last bit.
      - unary `-` (`.negatable`), `sqrt` and `cbrt` (`.squareRoot`, `.cubeRoot`, which halve
        or third every power and are compile errors when a power does not divide).

      A quantity renders through `show`, which needs a `Decimalizer` in scope and writes the
      value, the unit symbols joined with `·`, and superscript powers (`8.54 m·s¯¹`); a
      `Prefixes` given for the units scales the number to a metric prefix instead
      (`5.00 kB`). `toString` is the `Double`'s and loses the units, so `show` is the only
      correct rendering. The arithmetic between a plain number and a quantity relies on the
      compiler's `into` conversions, so `import scala.language.experimental.into` is needed
      alongside `import soundness.*`.

      The constructor and the companion's `units`, `unitsMap` and `dimension` take the measure
      as a type argument; the same names on an instance — `convert`, `normalize`, `invert`,
      `express`, `units`, `dimension`, `sqrt`, `cbrt` — are extension methods inherited by the
      companion from `internal2.Protoquantity`, and `value`, `underlying` and `amount` are
      extensions declared in `quantitative.internal`. All of them are available on any
      quantity with `import soundness.*`.

      Its companion's members are declared in `internal.Quantity`, from which the type is
      exported, and documented there.
  refer quantitative.Units
  refer quantitative.Amount
  refer quantitative.MetricUnit
  refer quantitative.Normalizable
  refer quantitative.Prefixes

entry .MetricUnit   a unit value the metric prefixes apply to: `Kilo(Metre)`, `Milli(Litre)`
    `MetricUnit[units]` is the opaque type `quantitative.internal.MetricUnit`, exported here:
    a `Quantity[units]` (it is a subtype) which is additionally a *unit*, so that a
    `MetricPrefix` can be applied to it. `Metre`, `Gram`, `Second`, `Newton`, `Litre` and the
    other SI and CGS units are `MetricUnit`s; `Foot` and `Pound` are plain `Quantity`s, since
    `Kilo(Foot)` is not a unit anyone writes.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    sealed trait Information extends Dimension
    sealed trait Bits[power <: Nat] extends Units[power, Information]
    val Bit: MetricUnit[Bits[1]] = MetricUnit(1.0)
    given Designation[Bits[1]] = () => t"bit"

    5000*Kilo(Bit)
    ```

    `MetricUnit(value)` makes one from a `Double`, and `MetricUnit(quantity)` from a quantity
    (so `MetricUnit(Joule/Second)` is the watt). The companion's `underlying` given marks it as
    a `Double` underneath.

entry .Temperature   an absolute temperature, measured from absolute zero, shown on the scale in scope
  detail
      Temperature is measured from an arbitrary zero, so thirty degrees plus thirty degrees is
      not sixty degrees of anything: it is an *offset* quantity, and `Temperature` — the opaque
      type `quantitative.internal.Temperature`, a `Double` of kelvins — keeps it apart from
      the `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]` which measures a *difference* of temperature. A temperature is
      created on a scale, `Celsius(30)`, `Fahrenheit(86)`, `Kelvin(303.15)` (the last needs
      `import quantitative.*`, as its `apply` is not on the umbrella) or `Temperature(303.15)`,
      and shown on whichever `TemperatureScale` is imported from `temperatureScales`:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import scala.language.experimental.into

      given Decimalizer = Decimalizer(3)

      locally:
        import temperatureScales.celsiusScale
        Fahrenheit(100).show                          // t"37.8 °C"

      locally:
        import temperatureScales.fahrenheitScale
        (zero[Temperature] + 300*Kelvin).show         // t"80.3 °F"

      (Fahrenheit(100) - zero[Temperature]).convert[Rankines].show   // t"560 °R"
      ```

      A `Temperature` adds and subtracts a `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]` or a `Quantity[Rankines[1]]`
      to give another temperature, and the difference of two temperatures is a
      `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]`, which converts like any other quantity. Two temperatures compare
      with `<`, `>`, `<=` and `>=`, through the `orderable` given the companion inherits from
      `protointernal#Temperature2`. Multiplying two temperatures, or adding them, is
      meaningless and does not compile. The readings on each scale are the extensions
      `kelvin`, `celsius`, `fahrenheit` and `rankine`, declared in `quantitative.internal`.

      Its companion's members are declared in `internal.Temperature`, from which the type is
      exported, and documented there.
  refer quantitative.TemperatureScale
  refer quantitative.temperatureScales
  refer quantitative.Celsius

entry .Measure   the type of a quantity's units: one `Units` type or an intersection of several
    The upper bound of `Quantity`'s type argument. A single unit raised to a power is a
    `Units[power, dimension]`, which is a `Measure`; a compound measure is an intersection,
    `Metres[1] & Seconds[-2]`, in any order; and `Quantity[Measure]` itself is a dimensionless
    quantity. A `Measure` is pure (it extends `caps.Pure`), so it never carries a capture set.

entry .Units   one unit raised to a power, `Units[power, dimension]`: `Metres[1]`, `Seconds[-2]`
  detail
      Every unit is a type constructor over its power, a `Nat` (an `Int & Singleton`): `Metres`
      is the SI unit of `Distance`, and `Metres[2]` is square metres, `Metres[-1]` per metre.
      A `Measure` is one of these or an intersection of several. The SI base units are sealed
      traits here — `Metres`, `Kilograms`, `Seconds`, `Amperes`, `Kelvins`, `Candelas`,
      `Moles` — and `quantitative.units` adds the imperial, time and typographic ones (`Feet`,
      `Pounds`, `Hours`, `Points`, …).

      A unit of your own is a trait extending `Units[power, dimension]`, with a unit value and
      a `Designation` for its symbol; it may be sealed, since nothing implements it at
      runtime:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      trait CpuPerformance extends Dimension
      trait Flops[power <: Nat] extends Units[power, CpuPerformance]

      val Flop: MetricUnit[Flops[1]] = MetricUnit(1)
      given Designation[Flops[1]] = () => t"FLOPS"

      Mega(Flop)*Second   // Quantity[Flops[1] & Seconds[1]]
      ```
  refer quantitative.Measure
  refer quantitative.Dimension
  refer quantitative.Designation
  refer quantitative.Ratio

entry .Metres   the metre, the SI and principal unit of `Distance`: `Metres[power]`
    Symbol `m` (`Designation.metres`); the unit mixed-unit distances convert to.
entry .Kilograms   the kilogram, the SI and principal unit of `Mass`: `Kilograms[power]`
    Symbol `kg`: its `Designation` is `g` with the `Kilo` prefix, which is why the unit
    *value* is `Gram` (a thousandth of a kilogram) and one kilogram is written `Kilo(Gram)`.
    Masses always normalize to and display in kilograms.
entry .Seconds   the second, the SI and principal unit of `Time`: `Seconds[power]`
    Symbol `s`. `Seconds[1]` is also a `Radix.Regular`, so the SI second can be a rung of an
    aviation timespan or timestamp while remaining an ordinary unit.
entry .Amperes   the ampere, the SI and principal unit of `Current`: `Amperes[power]`
    Symbol `A`.
entry .Kelvins   the kelvin, the SI and principal unit of `Heat`: `Kelvins[power]`
    Symbol `K`. A `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]` is a *difference* of temperature; an absolute
    temperature is a `Temperature`.
entry .Candelas   the candela, the SI and principal unit of `Luminosity`: `Candelas[power]`
    Symbol `cd`.
entry .Moles   the mole, the SI and principal unit of `AmountOfSubstance`: `Moles[power]`
    Symbol `mol`.
entry .Rankines   the degree Rankine, a unit of `Heat`: `Rankines[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `°R`; one kelvin is 1.8 degrees Rankine (`.ratio`). Like the kelvin it is measured
      from absolute zero, so `Quantity[Rankines[1]]` is both a temperature difference and a
      quantity a `Temperature` adds and subtracts; `Rankine` is the matching
      `TemperatureScale`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `°R`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Rankines[1] & Kelvins[-1], 1.8]`: a kelvin is 1.8 degrees Rankine

entry .Dimension   what a unit measures: `Distance`, `Mass`, `Time`, `Heat`, `Current`, `Luminosity`, `AmountOfSubstance`
    The trait every dimension extends, each a sealed trait with no members: a `Units[power,
    dimension]` type names one, and the dimensional analysis compares quantities by their
    dimensions and powers rather than by their units. The seven SI base dimensions are
    declared here, each with a `Principal` unit; `Typometry` (the typographic em) is an eighth
    in `quantitative.units`. A dimension of your own is a trait extending `Dimension`, with a
    `Units` type and, if its quantities are to be named, an `Amount` given. A `Dimension` is
    pure, so it never carries a capture set.
entry .Distance   the dimension of length; principal unit `Metres`
entry .Mass   the dimension of mass; principal unit `Kilograms`
entry .Time   the dimension of time; principal unit `Seconds`
entry .Heat   the dimension of temperature; principal unit `Kelvins`
entry .Current   the dimension of electric current; principal unit `Amperes`
entry .Luminosity   the dimension of luminous intensity; principal unit `Candelas`
entry .AmountOfSubstance   the dimension of amount of substance; principal unit `Moles`

entry .Principal   which unit a dimension's mixed-unit results take: the SI unit of each base dimension
  detail
      `Principal[dimension, unit]` names the unit which a quantity in that dimension
      normalizes to when two units meet: `2*Metre + 2*Foot` and `2*Foot + 2*Metre` are both in
      metres, and `(2*Metre)*(3*Foot)` is in square metres, because `Principal.distance`
      names `Metres`. It also mediates conversion: a `Ratio` between two non-principal units
      need not exist, since each can be converted through the principal one. The seven givens
      are the SI base units; a dimension of your own with more than one unit needs a given
      `Principal[MyDimension, MyUnits]()` in scope, or arithmetic between its units fails with
      _the operands both represent the physical quantity …, but there is no principal unit
      specified for this dimension_.
  entry .distance   `Metres` is principal for `Distance`
  entry .mass   `Kilograms` is principal for `Mass`
  entry .time   `Seconds` is principal for `Time`
  entry .current   `Amperes` is principal for `Current`
  entry .luminosity   `Candelas` is principal for `Luminosity`
  entry .heat   `Kelvins` is principal for `Heat`
  entry .amountOfSubstance   `Moles` is principal for `AmountOfSubstance`

entry .Ratio   the conversion rate between two units of one dimension, as a singleton `Double` type
    `Ratio[From[1] & To[-1], rate]` says that one `To` is `rate` of `From`: `Ratio[Metres[1] &
    Feet[-1], 0.3048]` (or equally `Ratio[Feet[-1] & Metres[1], 0.3048]`, the order of the
    intersection being immaterial) says a foot is 0.3048 metres. The rate is a *type*, a
    `Double & Singleton` literal, so the given is erased and the conversion folds into the
    one multiplication it denotes; every `ratio` given in `quantitative.units` is declared
    `erased`. The arithmetic macros find a ratio by implicit scope, trying the pair in both
    orders and otherwise routing through the dimension's `Principal` unit, and raising a
    compile error when none exists: _both operands represent the physical quantity distance,
    but the conversion ratio between them is not known. To provide the conversion ratio,
    please provide a contextual instance in scope, with the type `Ratio[Feet[1] &
    Metres[-1]]` or `Ratio[Metres[1] & Feet[-1]]`._

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    sealed trait Cubits[power <: Nat] extends Units[power, Distance]
    val Cubit: Quantity[Cubits[1]] = Quantity(1.0)
    given Designation[Cubits[1]] = () => t"cbt"
    inline given Ratio[Metres[1] & Cubits[-1], 0.4572] = !!

    2*Cubit + Metre   // 1.91 m
    ```

entry .Designation   the symbol a unit renders with: `m`, `kg`, `ft`
  detail
      `Designation[Units[1]]` is the typeclass `show` consults for each unit of a quantity's
      measure; `name()` is the symbol and `siPrefix` the prefix it is written with, `NoPrefix`
      for every unit but the kilogram. `text` is the two together. The seven SI base units'
      givens are here, and every unit in `quantitative.units` carries its own in its
      companion. A unit with no `Designation` renders by its type's name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Designation[Flops[1]] = () => t"FLOPS"
      ```
  entry .metres   `m`
  entry .kilograms   `kg`: the symbol `g` with the `Kilo` prefix
  entry .seconds   `s`
  entry .amperes   `A`
  entry .kelvins   `K`
  entry .candelas   `cd`
  entry .moles   `mol`
  entry #name   the unit's symbol, without any prefix
  entry #siPrefix   the metric prefix the unit is conventionally written with; `NoPrefix` by default
  entry #text   the prefix symbol and the unit symbol together

entry .Redesignation   a single symbol for a whole derived measure: `J` for kg·m²·s⁻²
  detail
      Where a `Designation` names one unit, a `Redesignation[measure]` names a combination:
      with `Redesignation.joules` in scope, `(45*Joule).show` is `45.0 J` rather than
      `45.0 kg·m²·s¯²`. The lookup is by the exact measure, so a force in newtons shows as `N`
      and an energy as `J`, but a pressure (`Pascal`) still shows in base units. A derived
      unit of your own takes one as `given Redesignation[Metres[2] & Seconds[-1]](t"St")`.
  entry .joules   `J` for `Kilograms[1] & Metres[2] & Seconds[-2]`
  entry .newtons   `N` for `Kilograms[1] & Metres[1] & Seconds[-2]`
  entry #name   the symbol

entry .Amount   the physical name of a combination of dimensions: `velocity`, `energy`, `chemical potential`
  detail
      `Amount[measure, label]` pairs a measure of base-unit powers with the name of the
      physical quantity it represents. The givens are erased evidence read by macros alone:
      `dimension` reports a quantity's name through them, and the compile error for a
      mismatched sum names both sides through them (_the left operand represents distance,
      but the right operand represents energy_). `Amount[units]` is the name of a measure as a
      macro, a compile error when none is known. The companion also holds a type alias for
      every named derived measure — `Amount.Velocity`, `Amount.Force`, `Amount.Energy` — in
      terms of `Units[power, Dimension]`, usable as the type of a quantity.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def kineticEnergy(mass: Quantity[Kilograms[1]], speed: Quantity[Amount.Velocity])
      :   Quantity[Amount.Energy] =
        0.5*mass*speed*speed
      ```

      A quantity of your own dimension is named by declaring one:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      sealed trait Information extends Dimension
      erased given Amount[Units[1, Information], "information"] = !!
      ```

      The seven base dimensions are `distance`, `mass`, `time`, `current`, `temperature`,
      `luminosity` and `amount of substance`; the derived names follow the list of physical
      quantities, with `Typometry.quantity` adding `em` in `quantitative.units`.
  entry .apply   `Amount[units]`: the name of a measure, as text; a compile error when it has none
  entry .distance   `distance`, `Units[1, Distance]`
  entry .mass   `mass`, `Units[1, Mass]`
  entry .time   `time`, `Units[1, Time]`
  entry .current   `current`, `Units[1, Current]`
  entry .heat   `temperature`, `Units[1, Heat]`
  entry .luminosity   `luminosity`, `Units[1, Luminosity]`
  entry .substance   `amount of substance`, `Units[1, AmountOfSubstance]`
  entry .Absement   mass·time
  entry .absement   `absement`
  entry .AbsorbedDoseRate   distance²·time⁻³
  entry .absorbedDoseRate   `absorbed dose rate`
  entry .Acceleration   distance·time⁻²
  entry .acceleration   `acceleration`
  entry .Area   distance²
  entry .area   `area`
  entry .AreaDensity   distance⁻²·mass
  entry .areaDensity   `area density`
  entry .Capacitance   distance⁻²·mass⁻¹·time⁴·current
  entry .capacitance   `capacitance`
  entry .ChemicalPotential   distance²·mass·time⁻²·substance⁻¹
  entry .chemicalPotential   `chemical potential`
  entry .Crackle   distance·time⁻⁵
  entry .crackle   `crackle`
  entry .CurrentDensity   distance⁻²·current
  entry .currentDensity   `current density`
  entry .DynamicViscosity   distance⁻¹·mass·time⁻¹
  entry .dynamicViscosity   `dynamic viscosity`
  entry .ElectricCharge   time·current
  entry .electricCharge   `electric charge`
  entry .ElectricChargeDensity   distance⁻³·time·current
  entry .electricChargeDensity   `electric charge density`
  entry .ElectricDipoleMoment   distance·time·current
  entry .electricDipoleMoment   `electric dipole moment`
  entry .ElectricDisplacementField   distance⁻²·time·current
  entry .electricDisplacementField   `electric displacement field`
  entry .ElectricFieldStrength   distance·mass·time⁻³·current⁻¹
  entry .electricFieldStrength   `electric field strength`
  entry .ElectricalConductance   distance⁻²·mass⁻¹·time³·current²
  entry .electricalConductance   `electric conductance`
  entry .ElectricalConductivity   distance⁻³·mass⁻¹·time³·current²
  entry .electricalConductivity   `electric conductivity`
  entry .ElectricalPotential   distance²·mass·time⁻³·current⁻¹
  entry .electricalPotential   `electric potential`
  entry .ElectricalResistivity   distance³·mass·time⁻³·current⁻²
  entry .electricalResistivity   `electric resistivity`
  entry .Energy   distance²·mass·time⁻²
  entry .energy   `energy`
  entry .Entropy   distance²·mass·time⁻²·temperature⁻¹
  entry .entropy   `entropy`
  entry .Force   distance·mass·time⁻²
  entry .force   `force`
  entry .Frequency   time⁻¹
  entry .frequency   `frequency`
  entry .Illuminance   distance⁻²·luminosity
  entry .illuminance   `illuminance`
  entry .Impedance   distance²·mass·time⁻³·current⁻²
  entry .impedance   `impedance`
  entry .Inductance   distance²·mass·time⁻²·current⁻²
  entry .inductance   `inductance`
  entry .Jerk   distance·time⁻³
  entry .jerk   `jerk`
  entry .Jounce   distance·time⁻⁴
  entry .jounce   `jounce`
  entry .LinearDensity   distance⁻¹·mass
  entry .linearDensity   `linear density`
  entry .MagneticFlux   distance²·mass·time⁻²·current⁻¹
