entry .Normalizable   the typeclass behind `normalize`: the ratio from one measure to another of the same dimensions
  detail
      `source is Normalizable to result` carries the `ratio()` by which a quantity in `source`
      units is divided to express it in `result` units, and `normalize` applies it. The
      instance is derived by a macro (`.derived`) from the `Ratio` givens in scope, so it is
      never written by hand; a `result` of different dimensionality is a compile error, _the
      left operand represents …, but the right operand represents …; these are incompatible
      physical quantities_. Any measure `Normalizable to Seconds[1]` is accepted as a duration
      by `Quantity.genericDuration`.
  entry .derived   the macro-derived instance for any two measures of one dimensionality
  entry #ratio   the factor between the two measures, as a `Double`
  entry #normalize   a quantity in the source units as a quantity in the result units

entry .Quantifiable   makes a type of your own into a quantity: `value.quantify`
    `Quantifiable[quantity, units]` is a single-method typeclass whose extension `quantify`
    turns a value into a `Quantity[units]`, after which it takes part in dimensional
    arithmetic and comparison. A typographic point, one seventy-second of an inch, becomes a
    genuine length:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Pts(value: Double)
    given Quantifiable[Pts, Inches[1]] = pts => (Inch*pts.value)/72.0

    Pts(71).quantify < Inch   // true
    ```

entry .Distributive   splits a quantity into its numeric part and the units it is placed back with
  detail
      A typeclass for the renderers which lay numbers out in columns: `parts` gives the
      numeric components of a value (one, for a quantity: its `Double`), and `place` puts the
      formatted parts back into the surrounding text, which for a quantity is the number
      followed by its unit symbols. `Distributive[measure](units)` builds one for a given
      units text, and the inline `.distributive` given derives it for any `Quantity[measure]`
      from `Quantity.units`.
  entry .apply   `Distributive[measure](units)`: an instance which places the number before the given units text
  entry .distributive   the instance for any `Quantity[measure]`, with the measure's own unit symbols
  entry #parts   the numeric parts of a value
  entry #place   the formatted parts placed back into text

entry .MetricPrefix   a metric prefix, applied to a `MetricUnit` to scale it: `Kilo(Metre)`, `Mebi(Byte)`
  detail
      A `MetricPrefix` is a name, a symbol, an exponent and a base, `10` for the SI prefixes
      and `2` for the binary ones, and `apply` scales a `MetricUnit` by `base` to the
      `exponent`: `15*Kilo(Metre)` is 15000 m, `1.5*Milli(Metre)` is 0.0015 m, `10*Kibi(Metre)`
      is 10240 m. Every SI prefix from `Quecto` (10⁻³⁰) to `Quetta` (10³⁰) is an object here,
      with the eight binary prefixes `Kibi` to `Yobi`, and `NoPrefix` for none. The prefix
      applies only to a `MetricUnit`, not to any `Quantity`: `Kilo(Metre)` is a unit, but
      `Kilo(5*Metre)` does not compile. A `Prefixes` list of them lets `show` choose one for
      display.

      The prefixes, with their symbols and the factors they denote:

      ```tabular
      column  Prefix
      column  Symbol
      column  Factor
      row  `Quetta`  `Q`  10³⁰
      row  `Ronna`  `R`  10²⁷
      row  `Yotta`  `Y`  10²⁴
      row  `Zetta`  `Z`  10²¹
      row  `Exa`  `E`  10¹⁸
      row  `Peta`  `P`  10¹⁵
      row  `Tera`  `T`  10¹²
      row  `Giga`  `G`  10⁹
      row  `Mega`  `M`  10⁶
      row  `Kilo`  `k`  10³
      row  `Hecto`  `h`  10²
      row  `Deka`  `da`  10¹
      row  `Deci`  `d`  10⁻¹
      row  `Centi`  `c`  10⁻²
      row  `Milli`  `m`  10⁻³
      row  `Micro`  `µ`  10⁻⁶
      row  `Nano`  `n`  10⁻⁹
      row  `Pico`  `p`  10⁻¹²
      row  `Femto`  `f`  10⁻¹⁵
      row  `Atto`  `a`  10⁻¹⁸
      row  `Zepto`  `z`  10⁻²¹
      row  `Yocto`  `y`  10⁻²⁴
      row  `Ronto`  `r`  10⁻²⁷
      row  `Quecto`  `q`  10⁻³⁰
      row  `Kibi`  `Ki`  2¹⁰
      row  `Mebi`  `Mi`  2²⁰
      row  `Gibi`  `Gi`  2³⁰
      row  `Tebi`  `Ti`  2⁴⁰
      row  `Pebi`  `Pi`  2⁵⁰
      row  `Exbi`  `Ei`  2⁶⁰
      row  `Zebi`  `Zi`  2⁷⁰
      row  `Yobi`  `Yi`  2⁸⁰
      ```

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      15*Kilo(Metre)      // 15000 m
      10*Mebi(Metre)      // 10485760 m
      Kilo.symbol         // "k"
      ```
  refer quantitative.Prefixes
  refer quantitative.MetricUnit
  entry #apply   `prefix(unit)`: the unit scaled by `base` to the `exponent`, as a `Quantity`
  entry #name   the prefix's name: `kilo`
  entry #symbol   the prefix's symbol, written before the unit's: `k`
  entry #exponent   the power of `base` the prefix denotes: `3` for `Kilo`, `10` for `Kibi`
  entry #base   `10` for an SI prefix, `2` for a binary one
entry .NoPrefix   no prefix: exponent 0, empty symbol
entry .Deka   `da`, 10¹
entry .Hecto   `h`, 10²
entry .Kilo   `k`, 10³
entry .Mega   `M`, 10⁶
entry .Giga   `G`, 10⁹
entry .Tera   `T`, 10¹²
entry .Peta   `P`, 10¹⁵
entry .Exa   `E`, 10¹⁸
entry .Zetta   `Z`, 10²¹
entry .Yotta   `Y`, 10²⁴
entry .Ronna   `R`, 10²⁷
entry .Quetta   `Q`, 10³⁰
entry .Deci   `d`, 10⁻¹
entry .Centi   `c`, 10⁻²
entry .Milli   `m`, 10⁻³
entry .Micro   `µ`, 10⁻⁶
entry .Nano   `n`, 10⁻⁹
entry .Pico   `p`, 10⁻¹²
entry .Femto   `f`, 10⁻¹⁵
entry .Atto   `a`, 10⁻¹⁸
entry .Zepto   `z`, 10⁻²¹
entry .Yocto   `y`, 10⁻²⁴
entry .Ronto   `r`, 10⁻²⁷
entry .Quecto   `q`, 10⁻³⁰
entry .Kibi   `Ki`, 2¹⁰
entry .Mebi   `Mi`, 2²⁰
entry .Gibi   `Gi`, 2³⁰
entry .Tebi   `Ti`, 2⁴⁰
entry .Pebi   `Pi`, 2⁵⁰
entry .Exbi   `Ei`, 2⁶⁰
entry .Zebi   `Zi`, 2⁷⁰
entry .Yobi   `Yi`, 2⁸⁰

entry .Prefixes   the metric prefixes `show` may scale a quantity to, as a given for its units
  detail
      Without one, a large or small quantity shows in scientific notation (`5.00×10³ B`). A
      `Prefixes on units` given in scope lists the prefixes to choose from, and `show` picks
      the largest whose scaled value is at least `minimum` (1.0 by default), writing its
      symbol before the units:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Decimalizer = Decimalizer(3)

      sealed trait Information extends Dimension
      sealed trait Bytes[power <: Nat] extends Units[power, Information]
      val Byte: MetricUnit[Bytes[1]] = MetricUnit(1.0)
      given Designation[Bytes[1]] = () => t"B"

      locally:
        given Prefixes on Bytes[1] = Prefixes(List(Kilo, Mega, Giga, Tera))
        (5000*Byte).show            // t"5.00 kB"
        (1.5e9*Byte).show           // t"1.50 GB"
        (500*Byte).show             // t"500 B"

      locally:
        given Prefixes on Bytes[1] = Prefixes(List(Kibi, Mebi, Gibi))
        (5_000_000*Byte).show       // t"4.77 MiB"

      locally:
        given Prefixes on Bytes[1] = Prefixes(List(Kilo, Mega), 0.1)
        (100*Byte).show             // t"0.100 kB"
      ```

      The prefix is chosen by the value's magnitude, so a negative value scales as its
      absolute value does; zero takes no prefix; and a value below the floor with no smaller
      prefix available falls through to the smallest exponent listed. The given's plane is
      the *whole* measure, so `Prefixes on Bytes[1] & Seconds[-1]` scales a rate as a whole
      (`1.50 GB·s¯¹`); a plain `Prefixes` given with no plane applies to every quantity.
  entry .apply   `Prefixes(prefixes, minimum = 1.0)`: a list of prefixes for a units type, with the floor for scaling
  entry #prefixes   the prefixes to choose from
  entry #minimum   the smallest scaled value a prefix may produce, 1.0 by default
  entry #select   the prefix for a value: the largest whose scaled value reaches `minimum`, or `NoPrefix` for zero

entry .Radix   the marker a unit carries to be one rung of a mixed-radix system such as a clock or calendar
  detail
      A `Radix` is a unit which can appear in the phantom type of an aviation timespan or
      timestamp. `Regular` radices have a constant ratio to the rung below them (seconds,
      minutes, hours, days); `Irregular` ones have a position-dependent length (months,
      years) and so cannot be added to a bare instant. `Seconds[1]` is declared `Radix.Regular`
      here so that the SI second can be a rung while remaining an ordinary unit; the other
      radices are aviation's.
  entry .Regular   a radix with a constant ratio to the one below it
  entry .Irregular   a radix whose length depends on its position, such as a month

entry .TemperatureScale   a scale a `Temperature` is read on: its suffix and its reading
  detail
      The trait the four scales implement — `Celsius`, `Fahrenheit`, `Rankine`, and the kelvin
      scale given in `temperatureScales` — and the given `Temperature.showable` needs in scope:
      `suffix` is written after the reading (`°C`), `kelvin(temperature)` is the reading of a
      temperature *on this scale* (despite its name: `Celsius.kelvin(Celsius(30))` is 30.0),
      and `unapply` lets a scale be used as an extractor, `case Celsius(reading) =>`. Each
      scale object also has an `apply` making a `Temperature` from a reading on it.
  refer quantitative.temperatureScales
  entry #suffix   the text written after a reading: `K`, `°C`, `°F`, `°R`
  entry #kelvin   the reading of a temperature on this scale, as a `Double`
  entry #unapply   extracts the reading on this scale: `case Celsius(degrees) =>`

entry .Celsius   the Celsius scale: `Celsius(30)` is a `Temperature`, shown as `30.0 °C` under `celsiusScale`
    Zero at the freezing point of water, 273.15 K. `Celsius(value)` makes an absolute
    `Temperature` from a Celsius reading, so `Celsius(30)` stores 303.15 kelvins; a reading
    of a `Temperature` in Celsius is `celsius`, from `quantitative.internal`, or the extractor
    `case Celsius(degrees)`.
entry .Fahrenheit   the Fahrenheit scale: `Fahrenheit(86)` is a `Temperature`, shown as `86.0 °F` under `fahrenheitScale`
    `Fahrenheit(value)` makes an absolute `Temperature` from a Fahrenheit reading
    (`Fahrenheit(32)` stores 273.15 kelvins); a reading in Fahrenheit is `fahrenheit`, or the
    extractor `case Fahrenheit(degrees)`.
entry .Rankine   the Rankine scale: absolute, in Fahrenheit-sized degrees, shown as `°R` under `rankineScale`
    `Rankine(value)` makes a `Temperature` from a Rankine reading (five ninths of it in
    kelvins); a reading in Rankine is `rankine`, or the extractor `case Rankine(degrees)`.
    Since the scale is absolute, `Rankines` is also a unit of temperature difference.

entry .temperatureScales   the scale a `Temperature` shows on, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `Temperature.showable` renders a temperature on the `TemperatureScale` in scope, and the
      choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is
      imported by name. The same temperature reads differently under each, with no
      conversion written by hand:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label On the Celsius scale
        code
            locally:
              import temperatureScales.celsiusScale
              Fahrenheit(100).show   // t"37.8 °C"
      frame
        label On the Fahrenheit scale
        code
            locally:
              import temperatureScales.fahrenheitScale
              Fahrenheit(100).show   // t"100 °F"
      frame
        label On the kelvin scale
        code
            locally:
              import temperatureScales.kelvinScale
              Fahrenheit(100).show   // t"311 K"
      frame
        label On the Rankine scale
        code
            locally:
              import temperatureScales.rankineScale
              Fahrenheit(100).show   // t"560 °R"
      ##
      ```
  entry .kelvinScale   readings in kelvins, suffix `K`
  entry .celsiusScale   readings in degrees Celsius, suffix `°C`; the `Celsius` object
  entry .fahrenheitScale   readings in degrees Fahrenheit, suffix `°F`; the `Fahrenheit` object
  entry .rankineScale   readings in degrees Rankine, suffix `°R`; the `Rankine` object

entry .apply   `Kelvin(value)`: an absolute `Temperature` from a reading in kelvins
    An extension on the `Kelvin` unit value, in `quantitative.units`, so that the kelvin reads
    like the other scales: `Kelvin(303.15)` is the same `Temperature` as `Celsius(30)`. It is
    deliberately not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where a top-level `apply` would
    collide with rudiments' own, so it needs `import quantitative.*`; `Temperature(303.15)`
    is the same value with no import.

entry .Metre   the metre, one `Metres[1]`: the SI unit of distance, a `MetricUnit`
    `5*Metre` is five metres; `Kilo(Metre)` a kilometre; `Metre*Metre` a square metre. Symbol
    `m`.
entry .Gram   the gram, a thousandth of a `Kilograms[1]`: the mass unit value, a `MetricUnit`
    The SI base unit of mass is the kilogram, itself a prefixed unit, so the value is the
    gram for consistency with every other unit and one kilogram is `Kilo(Gram)`. Masses
    always show in `kg`: `(10.4*Kilo(Gram)/Second).show` is `10.4 kg·s¯¹`.
entry .Second   the second, one `Seconds[1]`: the SI unit of time, a `MetricUnit`
    `2*Second`; `Metre/Second` a velocity; `Milli(Second)` a millisecond. Symbol `s`. The
    hour and minute as quantities are `Quantity[Hours[1]](1.0)` and `Quantity[Minutes[1]](1.0)`,
    since `Hour` and `Minute` as names are aviation's radices.
entry .Ampere   the ampere, one `Amperes[1]`: the SI unit of current, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Kelvin   the kelvin, one `Kelvins[1]`: the SI unit of temperature difference, a `MetricUnit`
    `300*Kelvin` is a `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]`, a *difference* which a `Temperature` adds and
    subtracts; `Kelvin(303.15)` (with `import quantitative.*`) is an absolute `Temperature`.
entry .Candela   the candela, one `Candelas[1]`: the SI unit of luminous intensity, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Mole   the mole, one `Moles[1]`: the SI unit of amount of substance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Em   the typographic em, one `Ems[1]`: a relative length with no ratio to any physical unit
    A `Quantity[Ems[1]]` in the `Typometry` dimension, shared by CSS and font metrics; it
    cannot be converted to a metre or a point, by design.

entry .Hertz   the hertz, one per second: the SI unit of frequency, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Newton   the newton, kg·m·s⁻²: the SI unit of force, a `MetricUnit`; shows as `N`
entry .Pascal   the pascal, one newton per square metre: the SI unit of pressure, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Joule   the joule, one newton-metre: the SI unit of energy, a `MetricUnit`; shows as `J`
entry .Calorie   the calorie, 4.184 joules, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Watt   the watt, one joule per second: the SI unit of power, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Coulomb   the coulomb, one ampere-second: the SI unit of electric charge, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Volt   the volt, one watt per ampere: the SI unit of electric potential, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Farad   the farad, one coulomb per volt: the SI unit of capacitance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Ohm   the ohm, one volt per ampere: the SI unit of resistance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Siemens   the siemens, one ampere per volt: the SI unit of conductance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Weber   the weber, one volt-second: the SI unit of magnetic flux, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Tesla   the tesla, one weber per square metre: the SI unit of magnetic flux density, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Henry   the henry, one weber per ampere: the SI unit of inductance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Lux   the lux, one candela per square metre: the SI unit of illuminance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Becquerel   the becquerel, one per second: the SI unit of radioactivity, a `MetricUnit`
    Dimensionally a frequency, so it is the same type as `Hertz`.
entry .Gray   the gray, one joule per kilogram: the SI unit of absorbed dose, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Sievert   the sievert, one joule per kilogram: the SI unit of equivalent dose, a `MetricUnit`
    The same type as `Gray`; the distinction is not dimensional.
entry .Katal   the katal, one mole per second: the SI unit of catalytic activity, a `MetricUnit`

entry .Dyne   the dyne, the CGS unit of force, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Erg   the erg, the CGS unit of energy, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Barye   the barye, a tenth of a pascal: the CGS unit of pressure, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Poise   the poise, a tenth of a pascal-second: the CGS unit of dynamic viscosity, a `MetricUnit`
    Defined as a tenth of a `Pascal`, so dimensionally a pressure rather than a viscosity.
entry .Galileo   the gal, a centimetre per second squared: the CGS unit of acceleration, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Stokes   the stokes, the CGS unit of kinematic viscosity, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Gauss   the gauss, the CGS unit of magnetic flux density, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Maxwell   the maxwell, the CGS unit of magnetic flux, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Oersted   the oersted, 79.577 amperes per metre: the CGS unit of magnetic field strength, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Gilbert   the gilbert, 0.796 amperes: the CGS unit of magnetomotive force, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Biot   the biot, ten amperes: the CGS unit of current, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Emu   the electromagnetic unit of magnetization, amperes per square metre, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Franklin   the franklin, 3.34×10⁻¹⁰ coulombs: the CGS unit of charge, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Debye   the debye, 3.335×10⁻³⁰ coulomb-metres: a unit of electric dipole moment, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Lambert   the lambert, the CGS unit of luminance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Phot   the phot, the CGS unit of illuminance, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Langley   the langley, 41840 joules per square metre, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Kayser   the kayser, a wavenumber of one per second as defined here, a `MetricUnit`
entry .Darcy   the darcy, 0.987×10⁻¹² square metres: a unit of permeability, a `MetricUnit`

entry .Inch   the inch, one `Inches[1]`
entry .Foot   the foot, one `Feet[1]`
    `3*Foot` converts to metres when it meets one: `2*Metre + 2*Foot` is 2.61 m.
entry .Yard   the yard, one `Yards[1]`
entry .Mile   the mile, one `Miles[1]`
    `Mile === 1760*Yard` is true.
entry .Furlong   the furlong, one `Furlongs[1]`
entry .NauticalMile   the nautical mile, one `NauticalMiles[1]`
entry .Lightyear   the light-year, one `Lightyears[1]`
entry .Grain   the grain, one `Grains[1]`
entry .Ounce   the ounce, one `Ounces[1]`
entry .Pound   the pound, one `Pounds[1]`
entry .Stone   the stone, one `Stones[1]`
entry .Hundredweight   the hundredweight, one `Hundredweights[1]`
entry .Ton   the long ton, one `Tons[1]`
entry .SolarDay   the solar day, one `Days[1]`
entry .Are   the are, a hundred square metres, a `MetricUnit[Metres[2]]`
entry .Acre   the acre, a tenth of a square furlong, a `Quantity[Furlongs[2]]`
entry .Litre   the litre, a thousandth of a cubic metre, a `MetricUnit[Metres[3]]`
    `Milli(Litre)` is the millilitre, from which the imperial volumes are defined.
entry .FluidOunce   the imperial fluid ounce, 28.41 millilitres, a `Quantity[Metres[3]]`
entry .Pint   the imperial pint, 568.26 millilitres, a `Quantity[Metres[3]]`
entry .Quart   the imperial quart, 1136.52 millilitres, a `Quantity[Metres[3]]`
entry .Gallon   the imperial gallon, 4546.09 millilitres, a `Quantity[Metres[3]]`

entry .Inches   the inch, an imperial unit of `Distance`: `Inches[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `in`; an inch is 0.0254 metres. Its unit value is `Inch`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `in`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Inches[-1] & Metres[1], 0.0254]`: an inch is 0.0254 metres
entry .Feet   the foot, an imperial unit of `Distance`: `Feet[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `ft`; a foot is 0.3048 metres. Its unit value is `Foot`, and `(3*Metre).convert[Feet]`
      is 9.84 ft.
  entry .designation   the symbol `ft`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Feet[-1] & Metres[1], 0.3048]`: a foot is 0.3048 metres
entry .Yards   the yard, an imperial unit of `Distance`: `Yards[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `yd`; a yard is 0.9144 metres. Its unit value is `Yard`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `yd`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Yards[-1] & Metres[1], 0.9144]`: a yard is 0.9144 metres
entry .Miles   the mile, an imperial unit of `Distance`: `Miles[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `mi`; a mile is 1609.344 metres. Its unit value is `Mile`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `mi`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Miles[-1] & Metres[1], 1609.344]`: a mile is 1609.344 metres
entry .Furlongs   the furlong, an imperial unit of `Distance`: `Furlongs[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `fur`; a furlong is 201.168 metres. Its unit value is `Furlong`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `fur`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Metres[1] & Furlongs[-1], 201.168]`: a furlong is 201.168 metres
entry .NauticalMiles   the nautical mile, a unit of `Distance`: `NauticalMiles[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `NM`; a nautical mile is 1852 metres. Its unit value is `NauticalMile`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `NM`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[NauticalMiles[1] & Metres[-1], 5.399568034557236E-4]`: a metre is that many nautical miles
entry .Lightyears   the light-year, a unit of `Distance`: `Lightyears[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `ly`. Its unit value is `Lightyear`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `ly`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Lightyears[1] & Metres[-1], 1.057E-16]`: a metre is 1.057×10⁻¹⁶ light-years
entry .Points   the typographic point, a unit of `Distance`: `Points[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `pt`; a point is a seventy-second of an inch, 0.3528 millimetres. A real length,
      convertible to inches and metres, unlike the relative `Ems`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `pt`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Points[-1] & Metres[1], 3.5277777777777776E-4]`: a point is that many metres
entry .Picas   the typographic pica, a unit of `Distance`: `Picas[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `pc`; a pica is twelve points, a sixth of an inch.
  entry .designation   the symbol `pc`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Picas[-1] & Metres[1], 0.004233333333333333]`: a pica is that many metres

entry .Grains   the grain, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Grains[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `gr`; a grain is 64.8 milligrams. Its unit value is `Grain`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `gr`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Grains[-1], 0.0000647989]`: a grain is that many kilograms
entry .Drams   the dram, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Drams[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `dr`; a dram is 1.772 grams. It has no unit value; one is
      `Quantity[Drams[1]](1.0)`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `dr`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Drams[-1], 0.00177184375]`: a dram is that many kilograms
entry .Ounces   the ounce, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Ounces[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `oz`; an ounce is 28.35 grams. Its unit value is `Ounce`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `oz`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Ounces[-1], 0.0283495]`: an ounce is that many kilograms
entry .Pounds   the pound, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Pounds[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `lb`; a pound is 0.4536 kilograms. Its unit value is `Pound`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `lb`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Pounds[-1], 0.453592]`: a pound is 0.453592 kilograms
entry .Stones   the stone, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Stones[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `st`; a stone is fourteen pounds, 6.35 kilograms. Its unit value is `Stone`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `st`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Stones[-1], 6.35029318]`: a stone is 6.35 kilograms
entry .Quarters   the quarter, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Quarters[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `qr`; a quarter is two stone, 12.7 kilograms. It has no unit value.
  entry .designation   the symbol `qr`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Quarters[-1], 12.700586360000001]`: a quarter is 12.7 kilograms
entry .Hundredweights   the hundredweight, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Hundredweights[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `cwt`; a hundredweight is eight stone, 50.8 kilograms. Its unit value is
      `Hundredweight`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `cwt`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Hundredweights[-1], 50.80234544]`: a hundredweight is 50.8 kilograms
entry .Tons   the long ton, an imperial unit of `Mass`: `Tons[power]`
  detail
      Twenty hundredweight, 1016 kilograms. Its unit value is `Ton`. It has no `Designation`,
      so it renders by its type's name.
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Kilograms[1] & Tons[-1], 1016.0469088]`: a ton is 1016 kilograms

entry .Minutes   the minute, a unit of `Time`: `Minutes[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `min`; sixty seconds. There is no `Minute` value here (the name is aviation's
      radix), so one minute is `Quantity[Minutes[1]](1.0)`, and `(60*Second).normalize[Minutes[1]]`
      is one of them.
  entry .designation   the symbol `min`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Seconds[1] & Minutes[-1], 60.0]`: a minute is sixty seconds
entry .Hours   the hour, a unit of `Time`: `Hours[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `h`; 3600 seconds. One hour is `Quantity[Hours[1]](1.0)`, and `10*Kilo(Metre)/Quantity[Hours[1]](1.0)`
      is a `Quantity[Metres[1] & Hours[-1]]`.
  entry .designation   the symbol `h`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Seconds[1] & Hours[-1], 3600.0]`: an hour is 3600 seconds
entry .Days   the solar day, a unit of `Time`: `Days[power]`
  detail
      86400 seconds. Its unit value is `SolarDay`. Its `designation` given is declared for
      `Hours[1]` rather than `Days[1]`, so a day currently renders by its type's name.
  entry .designation   the symbol `d`, declared (as the source stands) for `Hours[1]`
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Seconds[1] & Days[-1], 86400.0]`: a day is 86400 seconds
entry .SiderealDays   the sidereal day, a unit of `Time`: `SiderealDays[power]`
  detail
      86164.0905 seconds, the Earth's rotation relative to the stars. No unit value and no
      `Designation`.
  entry .ratio   `Ratio[Seconds[1] & SiderealDays[-1], 86164.0905]`: a sidereal day is 86164.0905 seconds

entry .Typometry   the dimension of the typographic em, with no ratio to any physical length
  detail
      A sealed `Dimension` of its own, so that a length in ems — a relative unit, shared by
      CSS and font metrics — is a `Quantity[Ems[1]]` which cannot be added to or converted
      into a physical length. `quantity` names its amount `em`.
  entry .quantity   `Amount[Units[1, Typometry], "em"]`: the name of an em quantity
entry .Ems   the em, the unit of `Typometry`: `Ems[power]`
  detail
      Symbol `em`. Its unit value is `Em`. There is no `Ratio` from it to any other unit, by
      design.
  entry .designation   the symbol `em`

entry .constants   the physical constants, as quantities with their units
  detail
      Each is a `val` of the appropriate `Quantity` type, so `constants.SpeedOfLightInVacuum`
      is a `Quantity[Metres[1] & Seconds[-1]]` which shows as `3.00×10⁸ m·s¯¹`, and
      `PlanckConstant` as `6.63×10¯³⁴ m²·kg·s¯¹`. The package merges with the umbrella's own,
      so `constants.PlanckConstant` works under `import soundness.*`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import scala.language.experimental.into

      given Decimalizer = Decimalizer(3)

      val mass = 2*Kilo(Gram)
      (mass*constants.SpeedOfLightInVacuum*constants.SpeedOfLightInVacuum).show   // t"1.80×10¹⁷ J"
      ```
  entry .SpeedOfLightInVacuum   c, 299792458 m·s⁻¹
  entry .MagneticConstant   μ₀, the vacuum permeability, in N·A⁻²
  entry .ElectricConstant   ε₀, the vacuum permittivity, 8.854×10⁻¹² F·m⁻¹
  entry .CharacteristicImpedanceOfVacuum   Z₀, 376.73 Ω
  entry .PlanckConstant   h, 6.626×10⁻³⁴ m²·kg·s⁻¹
  entry .GravitationalConstant   G, 6.674×10⁻¹¹, in newtons per kilogram squared as defined here
  entry .ElementaryCharge   e, 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ C
  entry .AvogadroConstant   N_A, 6.022×10²³ mol⁻¹
  entry .BoltzmannConstant   k, the Boltzmann constant, in J·K⁻¹

entry .internal   the home of the opaque `Quantity`, `MetricUnit` and `Temperature` types and the unit macros
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the three opaque types have a scope in
      which their representation as a `Double` is visible, and so that the arithmetic macros
      (inherited from `protointernal`) have a stable home. `Quantity`, `MetricUnit` and
      `Temperature` are exported from here to the `quantitative` package, and their pages
      there have the full account. The extension methods declared here — `kelvin`, `celsius`,
      `fahrenheit` and `rankine` on a `Temperature`; `underlying`, `value` and `amount` on a
      `Quantity` — are available with `import soundness.*`.
  entry .Quantity   the opaque `Quantity[units] = Double`; see `quantitative.Quantity`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `Quantity`, exported with it to the `quantitative`
        package.
    entry .apply   `Quantity[units](value)`: a quantity of the given units from a `Double`
        The only way to make a quantity from a bare number, since a `Double` does not convert
        implicitly: `Quantity[Metres[1]](5.0)` is five metres, and `Quantity(1)/(2*Metre/Second)`
        divides a dimensionless quantity by a speed. Inline, so it costs nothing.
    entry .units   the unit symbols of a measure, as text: `Quantity.units[units]`
        `Quantity.units[Metres[1] & Seconds[-2]]` is the text `m·s¯²`, the symbols found
        through each unit's `Designation` given (or a `Redesignation` for the whole measure, so
        `Kilograms[1] & Metres[2] & Seconds[-2]` is `J`). A macro, resolved as the code
        compiles. `quantity.units` on an instance, the symbol-to-power map, is the extension
        `internal2.Protoquantity#units`.
    entry .unitsMap   the symbol-to-power map of a measure, `Quantity.unitsMap[units]`
        The `Map[Text, Int]` of a measure's unit symbols and their powers, computed at compile
        time from the `Designation` givens; `expressUnits` renders it as text.
    entry .expressUnits   renders a symbol-to-power map as `m·s¯²`
        Joins the symbols with `·`, writing a power other than `1` as superscript digits with
        `¯` for a minus sign.
    entry .dimension   the physical name of a measure: `velocity`, `area`, `chemical potential`
        `Quantity.dimension[units]` is an `Optional[Text]`, `Unset` when no `Amount` given names
        the combination of dimensions. A macro which looks the measure's dimensionality up among
        the `Amount` givens, so a dimension of your own is named by declaring one.
        `quantity.dimension` on an instance is the extension `internal2.Protoquantity#dimension`.
    entry .zeroic   `zero[Quantity[units]]`: the zero quantity of any units
    entry .underlying   the `Underlying` given which lets a quantity stand in for a `Double`
        Marks `Quantity[units]` as a `Double` underneath, for the APIs which accept anything
        with that representation.
    entry .canEqual   permits `==` between two quantities of the same unit type, under strict equality
        Only quantities whose unit types match may be compared with `==`; two quantities of
        different units of one dimension have no `==` (under `import language.strictEquality`
        it does not compile), which is the gap `===` fills.
    entry .numeric   a `Numeric[Quantity[units]]`, the standard library's, for code written against it
    entry .addable   `+` between quantities of one dimension, converting to the principal unit
        A transparent inline given, expanded by a macro which checks the dimensions and computes
        the result type: `2*Metre + 2*Foot` and `2*Foot + 2*Metre` are both 2.61 m, the foot
        converted to the SI unit. Two different dimensions are a compile error naming both in
        words. `quantityAddOp` is the same operation as an `AddOp`, the form symbolism's `+`
        inlines to one `dadd`.
    entry .subtractable   `-` between quantities of one dimension, converting to the principal unit
        As `.addable`, for subtraction: `2*Metre - 2*Foot` is 1.39 m. `quantitySubOp` is its
        `SubOp` form.
    entry .multiplicable   `*` between two quantities: the units multiply, or cancel to a `Double`
        A transparent inline given: `2*Second*3*Metre` is a `Quantity[Metres[1] & Seconds[1]]`,
        and any dimension the two sides measure in different units is converted to the principal
        unit first, so `2*Metre*3*Foot` is 1.83 m². When every power cancels the result is a
        plain `Double`. `multiplicable2` to `multiplicable5` are `quantity*Double`,
        `Double*quantity`, `Int*quantity` and `quantity*Int`, each a `Quantity` of the same
        units, and `quantityMulOp` the `MulOp` form.
    entry .multiplicable2   `quantity*Double`, a quantity of the same units
    entry .multiplicable3   `Double*quantity`, a quantity of the same units
    entry .multiplicable4   `Int*quantity`, a quantity of the same units
    entry .multiplicable5   `quantity*Int`, a quantity of the same units
    entry .divisible   `/` between two quantities: the units divide, or cancel to a `Double`
        As `.multiplicable`, subtracting powers: `(2*Metre*Metre)/(3*Foot)` is 2.19 m, and
        `(20*Metre*Second)/(Metre*Second)` is the `Double` 20.0. `divisible2` and `divisible3`
        divide a `Double` or an `Int` by a quantity, inverting its units; `divisibleDouble` and
        `divisibleInt` divide a quantity by a plain number; `quantityDivOp` is the `DivOp` form.
    entry .divisible2   `Double/quantity`, with the quantity's units inverted
    entry .divisible3   `Int/quantity`, with the quantity's units inverted
    entry .divisibleDouble   `quantity/Double`, a quantity of the same units
    entry .divisibleInt   `quantity/Int`, a quantity of the same units
    entry .negatable   unary `-` on a quantity, a quantity of the same units
        Inlines to a single `dneg`.
    entry .squareRoot   `Rootable[2]`: the square root of a quantity, halving every power
        A transparent inline given behind `sqrt`; a compile error, _only quantities with units
        in even powers can have square roots calculated_, when a power is odd.
    entry .cubeRoot   `Rootable[3]`: the cube root of a quantity, dividing every power by three
    entry .commensurable   `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` between quantities of one dimension, converting as arithmetic does
        `6*Foot < 2*Metre` and `9*Foot*Foot < Metre*Metre` are both true. Comparing two
        different dimensions, or two powers of one dimension, is a compile error naming both:
        _the left operand represents distance, but the right operand represents area; these are
        incompatible physical quantities_.
    entry .checkable   `===` between quantities of one dimension: do they measure the same amount?
        `Mile === 1760*Yard` is true and `Mile === Inch` false; `Mile === Joule` does not
        compile. The comparison converts and is exact, so a conversion which introduces a
        rounding difference leaves two quantities that are equal on paper unequal.
    entry .quantityAddOp   the `AddOp` form of `.addable`, which symbolism's `+` inlines to a `dadd`
        A transparent inline given whose expansion is a `QuantityAddOp` instance; incompatible
        dimensions make the search fall through to `.addable`, which reports the error once.
    entry .quantitySubOp   the `SubOp` form of `.subtractable`
    entry .quantityMulOp   the `MulOp` form of `.multiplicable`, a `QuantityMulOp` or, if the units cancel, a `QuantityMulOpDouble`
    entry .quantityDivOp   the `DivOp` form of `.divisible`, a `QuantityDivOp` or, if the units cancel, a `QuantityDivOpDouble`
    entry .QuantityAddOp   the `AddOp` class behind `+`, with an inline `op` which is one `dadd`
        The class the transparent `quantityAddOp` given expands to, named so that the call
        site's static type carries the inline `op`; not something to construct by hand.
    entry .QuantitySubOp   the `SubOp` class behind `-`
    entry .QuantityMulOp   the `MulOp` class behind `*` when the product has units
    entry .QuantityMulOpDouble   the `MulOp` class behind `*` when the units cancel to a `Double`
    entry .QuantityDivOp   the `DivOp` class behind `/` when the quotient has units
    entry .QuantityDivOpDouble   the `DivOp` class behind `/` when the units cancel to a `Double`
    entry .showable   renders a quantity as `value units`, needing a `Decimalizer`
        `(7.567*Metre).show` is `7.57 m` with `Decimalizer(3)`, `(1.4*Metre*Metre).show` is
        `1.40 m²`, `(45*Joule).show` is `45.0 J`. Scales to a prefix when a `Prefixes` given is
        in scope; see `express`.
    entry .inspectable   the debug rendering of a quantity, the same as `show`
    entry .ShowableQuantity   the `Showable` class the `showable` given instantiates
    entry .InspectableQuantity   the `Inspectable` class the `inspectable` given instantiates
    entry .genericDuration   a quantity of time as a duration, in nanoseconds, for the APIs which take one
        Any `Quantity` whose units are `Normalizable to Seconds[1]` — seconds, minutes, hours,
        milliseconds — is `Abstractable across Durations to Long`, so it can be passed wherever
        a generic duration is accepted, such as a sleep or a timeout.
    entry .specificDuration   a quantity of time made from a duration in nanoseconds
        The reverse of `.genericDuration`: `Instantiable across Durations from Long`, giving a
        `Quantity` of the requested time units.
  entry .MetricUnit   the opaque `MetricUnit[units] <: Quantity[units]`; see `quantitative.MetricUnit`
  entry .Temperature   the opaque `Temperature = Double` of kelvins; see `quantitative.Temperature`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `Temperature`, exported with it to the `quantitative`
        package.
    entry .apply   `Temperature(kelvins)`: a temperature from its reading in kelvins
    entry .showable   renders a temperature on the `TemperatureScale` in scope: `30.0 °C`
        Needs a `TemperatureScale` given — one of the `temperatureScales` — and a `Decimalizer`;
        writes the reading on that scale followed by its suffix.
    entry .zeroic   `zero[Temperature]`: absolute zero
    entry .addable   `temperature + kelvins`: raises a temperature by a `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]`
    entry .addable2   `temperature + rankines`: raises a temperature by a `Quantity[Rankines[1]]`
    entry .subtractable   `temperature - kelvins`: lowers a temperature by a `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]`
    entry .subtractable2   `temperature - rankines`: lowers a temperature by a `Quantity[Rankines[1]]`
    entry .subtractable3   `temperature - temperature`: the difference, a `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]`
  entry .kelvin   a temperature's reading in kelvins
      `Celsius(30).kelvin` is 303.15: the stored value.
  entry .celsius   a temperature's reading in degrees Celsius: the kelvins less 273.15
  entry .fahrenheit   a temperature's reading in degrees Fahrenheit
  entry .rankine   a temperature's reading in degrees Rankine: the kelvins times nine fifths
  entry .underlying   the `Double` a quantity wraps, as a method
      `quantity.underlying` is the bare number with the units forgotten, which the macros use
      to build arithmetic; `value` is the inline form.
  entry .value   the `Double` a quantity wraps, inline
      `(5*Metre).value` is 5.0, with no unit; the only way to get the number out. Inline, so
      it costs nothing.
  entry .amount   `quantity.amount[name]`: the physical name of a quantity's measure, as a macro
      As `Amount[units]`: the name from the `Amount` givens, or a compile error, _the name of
      this amount is not known_.

entry .internal2   the trait of extension methods the `Quantity` companion inherits
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Protoquantity` holds the instance-side operations on a quantity —
      `convert`, `invert`, `normalize`, `sqrt`, `cbrt`, `units`, `express`, `dimension` — as
      extension methods, which `quantitative.internal.Quantity` extends so that they resolve
      from the companion.
  entry .Protoquantity   the extension methods `convert`, `invert`, `normalize`, `sqrt`, `cbrt`, `units`, `express` and `dimension`
    entry #convert   `quantity.convert[Feet]`: the same quantity in another unit of one of its dimensions
        Takes a unit type constructor — `Feet`, `Metres`, `Rankines` — and rescales every
        component of the quantity measured in that unit's dimension to it, leaving components of
        other dimensions as they are: `(3*Foot).convert[Metres]` is 0.914 m, and
        `(π*Metre*Metre).convert[Feet]` is in square feet. Naming a unit of a dimension the
        quantity does not have is not an error; it does nothing. The ratio comes from a `Ratio`
        given, directly or through the dimension's principal unit, and a missing one is a
        compile error, _both operands represent the physical quantity distance, but the
        conversion ratio between them is not known_. Transparent inline: the result type is
        computed as the code compiles.
    entry #normalize   `quantity.normalize[Seconds[1]]`: converts one dimension to a unit written with its power
        As `convert`, but the target is a fully-powered `Units` type, resolved through a
        `Normalizable` given: `(2*Hour).normalize[Seconds[1]]` is 7200 s, and
        `(1*Inch).normalize[Metres[1]]` is 0.0254 m. Components of other dimensions are
        untouched.
    entry #invert   the reciprocal, with every power negated
        `(2*Metre/Second).invert` is half a second per metre, a `Quantity[Seconds[1] &
        Metres[-1]]`. Transparent inline.
    entry #express   the quantity as text, scaled to a metric prefix if a `Prefixes` is in scope
        What `show` calls. With a `Prefixes on units` given (or any `Prefixes`) in scope the
        value is divided by the chosen prefix's factor and the prefix symbol is written before
        the units (`5.00 kB`); otherwise the value is written as the `Decimalizer` formats it,
        switching to scientific notation for very large or small numbers (`3.00×10⁸ m·s¯¹`).
    entry #sqrt   the square root, halving every power; a compile error when a power is odd
        `(4*Metre*Metre).sqrt` is 2 m. Resolved through `Quantity.squareRoot`, which rejects a
        measure with an odd power: _only quantities with units in even powers can have square
        roots calculated_.
    entry #cbrt   the cube root, dividing every power by three; a compile error when one does not divide
        Resolved through `Quantity.cubeRoot`: _only quantities with units whose powers are
        multiples of 3 can have cube roots calculated_.
    entry #units   the symbol-to-power map behind a quantity, as `Map[Text, Int]`
        `quantity.units` on an instance is the `Map[Text, Int]` from symbol to power behind it,
        `Map(t"m" -> 1, t"s" -> -2)`. A macro, resolved as the code compiles; the text form of
        a measure's units is `Quantity.units[units]`.
    entry #dimension   the physical name of a quantity's measure: `velocity`, `area`, `chemical potential`
        `quantity.dimension` on an instance is the `Text`, a compile error — _quantitative:
        there is no descriptive name for this physical quantity_ — when there is none. A macro
        which looks the measure's dimensionality up among the `Amount` givens, as
        `Quantity.dimension[units]` does.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Metre.dimension                                         // t"distance"
        (Metre/Second).dimension                                // t"velocity"
        (Foot*Foot*Kilo(Gram)/(Second*Second*Mole)).dimension   // t"chemical potential"
        ```

entry .protointernal   the compile-time unit algebra behind every quantity operation
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the trait `quantitative.internal` extends, holding the macro
      implementations. A measure is read into a `UnitsMap` from dimension to `UnitPower`;
      `normalize` converts the dimensions two maps share to one unit through the `Ratio` and
      `Principal` givens; `multiply`, `divide`, `add`, `sub`, `check` and `greaterThan` build
      the arithmetic from it; the `…Typeclass` and `make…Op` members expand the transparent
      inline givens of the `Quantity` companion; `collectUnits`, `describe`,
      `describeOptional` and `amount` are behind `units`, `dimension` and `Amount[units]`;
      `norm` is behind `convert`; `normalizable` derives `Normalizable`. `Temperature2` is the
      trait which gives `Temperature` its `orderable` given.
  entry #UnitPower   a unit reference and the power it is raised to
  entry #UnitsMap   a measure as a map from dimension to `UnitPower`, with `*` and `/` on it
  entry #Dimensionality   a map from dimension to power, which `quantityName` looks up among the `Amount` givens
  entry #UnitRef   a reference to a unit type, with its `designation` and its `dimensionRef`
  entry #DimensionRef   a reference to a dimension type, with its `principal` unit
  entry #Temperature2   the trait holding `Temperature`'s `orderable` given
    entry #orderable   `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` between two temperatures
  entry #readUnitPower   reads a `Units[power, dimension]` type into a `UnitPower`
  entry #normalizable   derives `source is Normalizable to result`, or reports incompatible dimensions
  entry #ratio   the conversion factor between two units, through a `Ratio` given or the principal unit
  entry #collectUnits   the symbol-to-power map of a measure, through `Designation` and `Redesignation`
  entry #multiply   the product of two quantities, with the units combined
  entry #divide   the quotient of two quantities, with the units combined
  entry #add   the sum of two quantities, or a compile error for different dimensions
  entry #sub   the difference of two quantities, or a compile error for different dimensions
  entry #check   `===` between two quantities, converting, or a compile error
  entry #greaterThan   `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` between two quantities, converting, or a compile error
  entry #amount   the physical name of a measure, or a compile error
  entry #describe   the physical name of a measure as text, or a compile error
  entry #describeOptional   the physical name of a measure, or `Unset`
  entry #norm   the expansion of `convert`: one dimension rescaled to the named unit
  entry #addTypeclass   the expansion of `Quantity.addable`
  entry #subTypeclass   the expansion of `Quantity.subtractable`
  entry #mulTypeclass   the expansion of `Quantity.multiplicable`
  entry #divTypeclass   the expansion of `Quantity.divisible`
  entry #divTypeclass2   the expansion of `Quantity.divisible2`, `Double/quantity`
  entry #divTypeclass3   the expansion of `Quantity.divisible3`, `Int/quantity`
  entry #checkable   the expansion of `Quantity.checkable`
  entry #sqrtTypeclass   the expansion of `Quantity.squareRoot`
  entry #cbrtTypeclass   the expansion of `Quantity.cubeRoot`
  entry #makeAddOp   the expansion of `Quantity.quantityAddOp`
  entry #makeSubOp   the expansion of `Quantity.quantitySubOp`
  entry #makeMulOp   the expansion of `Quantity.quantityMulOp`
  entry #makeDivOp   the expansion of `Quantity.quantityDivOp`
  entry #addQuantity   the body of `QuantityAddOp#op`
  entry #subQuantity   the body of `QuantitySubOp#op`
  entry #mulQuantity   the body of `QuantityMulOp#op`
  entry #mulQuantityDouble   the body of `QuantityMulOpDouble#op`
  entry #divQuantity   the body of `QuantityDivOp#op`
  entry #divQuantityDouble   the body of `QuantityDivOpDouble#op`
